# File Systems, Deadlocks, and Virtualization: Completing the OS Picture

> Files, deadlocks, and virtual machines turn a bare multitasking
> kernel into a dependable platform — and the cloud's foundation.

## 1. Files and Their Metadata

A **file** is a named, ordered collection of bytes on persistent
storage; the file system keeps each file's facts — not its content —
in a metadata record: on Unix, the **inode**.

| inode field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Size | Length in bytes |
| Timestamps | Created, modified, last accessed |
| Permissions | Owner, group, and world read/write/execute bits |
| Block pointers | Where the content lives (direct and indirect) |

[definition]
An **inode** stores a file's metadata and block pointers; the file
*name* lives in the directory entry — so one file can have several
names (hard links) pointing at a single inode.
[/definition]

## 2. Directories and Paths

A **directory** maps names to files and to other directories, forming
a tree; a **path** such as `/home/ada/report.txt` walks it from the
root. Typical operations: `create`, `open`, `read`, `write`, `seek`,
`close`, `delete`.

## 3. Allocating File Blocks

| Method | Layout | Strength | Weakness |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Contiguous | Consecutive blocks, like an array | Fast sequential *and* random access (base + index) | External fragmentation; files cannot grow |
| Linked | Each block points to the next | No external fragmentation; grows freely | Random access crawls along the chain; pointer overhead |
| Indexed | One index block lists all the file's blocks | Fast random access; no external fragmentation | Index block overhead; wasteful for tiny files |

The classic **FAT** (file allocation table) of DOS is an indexed
design kept separate from the files; Unix keeps the index inside
the inode with indirect pointers for big files.

## 4. Free Space and Crash Safety

| Free-space map | Idea | Trade-off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bitmap | One bit per block: 0 free, 1 used | Easy to find contiguous runs; large tables |
| Linked list | Free blocks chained together | Compact; slow to scan for runs |

[info title="Journaling in one line"]
A **journaling** file system (ext4, NTFS) writes the intended change
to a log first, then applies it — so a crash mid-write is repaired by
replaying the journal, not scanning the whole disk.
[/info]

## 5. Deadlock: The Four Coffman Conditions

**Deadlock** is a stand-off: processes each hold a resource and wait
for one another's — none can ever proceed. All four **Coffman
conditions** must hold simultaneously:

| Condition | Statement |
| --- | --- |
| Mutual exclusion | At most one process may use a resource at a time |
| Hold and wait | A process holds resources while waiting for more |
| No preemption | Resources cannot be forcibly taken away |
| Circular wait | A cycle of processes waits on each other |

[important]
Break any single condition and deadlock becomes impossible — the
insight driving every prevention strategy.
[/important]

## 6. Handling Deadlocks

| Strategy | Approach | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Prevention | Design away one Coffman condition | Request all resources at once (kills hold-and-wait); global numbering of resources (kills circular wait) |
| Avoidance | Grant requests only when the system stays in a **safe state** | Banker's algorithm needs each process's maximum claim in advance |
| Detection + recovery | Let deadlock happen, then fix it | Build the wait-for graph; find a cycle; kill a victim or roll it back |
| Ostrich algorithm | Ignore it and reboot if it ever happens | Roughly what Linux and Windows do — deadlocks are rare and prevention is expensive |

## 7. Virtual Machines and Hypervisors

A **virtual machine (VM)** is a software computer: virtual CPU, RAM,
and disks. The **hypervisor** creates and runs VMs — one physical
machine becomes many.

@diagram tree id="virtualization-stack" layout="tree" aria="A type 1 hypervisor runs directly on hardware and hosts three virtual machines"
  "Physical hardware (CPU, RAM, disk, NIC)" -> "Hypervisor"
  "Hypervisor" -> "VM 1: guest OS + apps"
  "Hypervisor" -> "VM 2: guest OS + apps"
  "Hypervisor" -> "VM 3: guest OS + apps"
@/diagram

| Type | Runs on | Examples | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Type 1 (bare metal) | Directly on hardware | VMware ESXi, Hyper-V, Xen, KVM | Data centres, cloud |
| Type 2 (hosted) | On top of a host OS | VirtualBox, VMware Workstation | Desktops, testing |

## 8. Containers vs VMs

A **container** packages an application with its libraries but shares
the *host kernel*: isolation comes from **namespaces** (what the
process may see) and **cgroups** (what it may use) — the Linux
primitives behind **Docker**.

| Property | Virtual machine | Container |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Boots in | Tens of seconds | Milliseconds |
| Size | Gigabytes (whole guest OS) | Megabytes (app + libraries) |
| Isolation | Hardware-level, very strong | Kernel-level, strong but weaker than VMs |
| Guest OS | Any OS on any host | Must share the host kernel |

[tip]
Containers are light, so dozens share one VM — VMs isolate tenants,
containers deploy fast inside them.
[/tip]

## 9. The Cloud Layer Cake

Cloud platforms stack these ideas. **IaaS** rents raw virtual machines
(you manage the OS up: e.g., EC2); **PaaS** rents a managed runtime
(you bring only code: e.g., Heroku); **SaaS** rents the finished
application itself (e.g., Gmail) — each level trading control for
convenience.

## Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| inode | File metadata + block pointers; names live in directories |
| Journaling | Log the change first; replay after a crash |
| Coffman conditions | Mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait |
| Container | Namespaces (see) + cgroups (use), sharing the host kernel |
| IaaS / PaaS / SaaS | Rent a VM / a platform / an application |
